<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Romulus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Romulus is the legendary founder and first king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, said to have been suckled with his twin brother Remus by a she-wolf. Roman tradition dated the founding of the city to 753 BCE, told how Romulus killed Remus, and credited him with the abduction of the Sabine women and a shared reign with the Sabine king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-tatius">Titus Tatius</a>. Historians do not regard him as a historical figure.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/romulus.jpg" alt="Romulus" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlo Brogi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brogi,_Carlo_(1850-1925)_-_n._8226_-_Certosa_di_Pavia_-_Medaglione_sullo_zoccolo_della_facciata.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In Roman tradition Romulus and Remus were the sons of Rhea Silvia by the god Mars. Their grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numitor">Numitor</a>, rightful king of Alba Longa, had been deposed by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amulius">Amulius</a>, who made Rhea Silvia a Vestal Virgin. When the twins were born, Amulius ordered them thrown into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>, but the river was swollen and the servants left them beneath a fig tree at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatine-hill">Palatine Hill</a>. A she-wolf suckled them until the royal herdsman Faustulus and his wife Acca Larentia found them. The brothers grew up among shepherds, and with the help of friends they killed Amulius and restored Numitor.</p><p>They then set out to found a city of their own and disagreed over the site. Remus saw six vultures over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aventine-hill">Aventine Hill</a>, Romulus twelve over the Palatine, and the quarrel ended with Romulus or one of his followers killing Remus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Romulus fortified the Palatine, ploughed a furrow for the city's boundary, and accepted the crown with the people's approval. He divided the people into three tribes, the Ramnes, Titienses and Luceres, chose one hundred men to form the Senate, and set up an asylum for fugitives on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitoline Hill</a>. Since the city had few women, he invited neighbouring peoples to a festival and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> seized the marriageable women among the Sabine guests. War followed. The Sabines under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-tatius">Titus Tatius</a> took the citadel through the treachery of Tarpeia, and fighting ended only when the Sabine women stepped between the armies. The two peoples formed one community, ruled jointly by Romulus and Tatius.</p><p>After Tatius was killed in a riot at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavinium">Lavinium</a>, Romulus is said to have conquered Fidenae and ravaged the countryside of Veii. The founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was commemorated each year on 21 April at the Parilia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After a reign of thirty-seven years Romulus is said to have disappeared in a storm while reviewing his troops on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campus-martius">Campus Martius</a>. Livy reports that he was either murdered by the senators or raised to heaven by Mars, and prefers the latter. He gained a cult, later assimilated to that of Quirinus, and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numa-pompilius">Numa Pompilius</a>, a Sabine.</p><p>How far a historical figure lies behind the legend is unclear and disputed. Most modern historians think the name Romulus is a back-formation from the name of the city. A rectangular tuff chest of the 6th century BCE, found by Giacomo Boni in 1899 beside the Lapis Niger and rediscovered in 2020, has been called the Tomb of Romulus, but no remains were found and it is more likely a cenotaph.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Romulus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Romulus</b> founded the city after consulting the gods through augury and was credited with the Consualia and the first temple of Jupiter Feretrius.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Roman writers placed the city's foundation by <b>Romulus</b> on the Palatine on 21 April 753 BCE, a date fixed by the scholar Varro.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-21">21 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He then deposed the young emperor <b>Romulus</b>, a takeover traditionally seen as the end of the Western Roman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-23">23 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The general Orestes installed his young son <b>Romulus</b> as emperor in the West.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-31">31 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Roman tradition tells of repeated wars from the time of <b>Romulus</b>, as Fidenae shifted between Rome and Veii.</blockquote><a href="/state/fidenae">Fidenae</a></li><li><blockquote>Tradition dated its founding by <b>Romulus</b> to 753 BCE; archaeology shows settlement from the 10th century BCE and the drainage and paving of the Forum around 650–600 BCE, when the villages became a single city.</blockquote><a href="/state/roman-kingdom">Roman Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Alba Longa, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.75,12.65" aria-label="Alba Longa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4858 677 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Romulus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5308 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5308 947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.75,12.65">What was here? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
